<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Senekerim-Hovhannes Artsruni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1026</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Senekerim-Hovhannes Artsruni was the last king of Vaspurakan, the Armenian kingdom around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a>, ruling from 1003. Under pressure from Turkic raids, he ceded his kingdom to the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> in 1021 in exchange for estates around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sebasteia</a>, the present-day Sivas, in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Thousands of his subjects followed him west, part of the Armenian movement into Cappadocia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> that preceded the Seljuk conquests.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Senekerim-Hovhannes, also known as Senekerim-John and in Byzantine sources simply as Senachereim, was the youngest son of Abusahl-Hamazasp of the Artsruni dynasty. When Abusahl died in 968, Vaspurakan was divided among his three sons. The eldest, Ashot-Sahak, kept the royal title and suzerainty over his younger brothers; after his death the second son, Gurgen-Khachik, usurped royal power and reigned as king until his own death in 1003.</p><p>In 1000, when the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> came east and annexed the principality of Tao, Senekerim and Gurgen visited him, paid him homage and received rich gifts in return. According to Stephen of Taron, Basil then sent letters to the neighbouring Muslim rulers declaring that Vaspurakan was under his protection and warning them to stop their raids. Matthew of Edessa reports instead that Basil concluded a treaty of alliance with the two brothers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On Gurgen's death Senekerim withheld power from his nephews and crowned himself king. His position on the throne remained insecure, partly because of the usurpation and partly because of the mounting raids of various <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> groups. Together with his relative, the Artsruni ruler of Andzevatsi, he offered to surrender his realm to the Empire in exchange for large domains in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. A devastating raid in 1018/19, in which the Armenian army proved unable to counter the Turkish horse archers, brought the cession about.</p><p>According to contemporary accounts the kingdom comprised 72 fortresses and 3,000 to 4,400 villages. It became the Byzantine theme of Asprakania, or Upper Media, with Basil Argyros as its first governor. In recompense Senekerim received the title of patrikios and the post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> of the theme of Cappadocia, together with the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sebasteia</a>, Larissa and Abara and many other settlements. His family and 14,000 retainers followed him west, and several of his relatives also received titles and gifts from the emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Senekerim died in 1025 or 1027; Matthew of Edessa wrongly places his death in 1029/30. He was succeeded by his eldest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>. His wife was a lady called Khushush, Chousousa in Greek, and they had four sons, David, Atom, Abu Sahl and Constantine, and apparently two daughters. One of them, Mariam of Vaspurakan, became the first wife of the Georgian king George I; the name of the other is recorded only in incomplete form.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Senekerim-Hovhannes Artsruni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Facing Seljuk raids, King <b>Senekerim-Hovhannes</b> ceded Vaspurakan to the Byzantine Empire in 1021 in exchange for estates around Sebasteia, and many Armenians followed him west.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-vaspurakan">Kingdom of Vaspurakan</a></li></ul></section>
